Turn a device's internet access on or off, on a schedule or on demand.

Adds a settings page under Firewall and a dashboard widget with per-device
toggles. Enforcement is one firewall rule against one externally managed alias
for the internet, two rules and two such aliases for messaging, and three rules
and three such aliases for gaming, so switching a device takes effect immediately
without a ruleset reload.

Blocked devices lose internet only - local services such as printers, media
players and dashboards keep working.

A device can also be denied messaging apps (WhatsApp, Telegram, Snapchat,
Signal, LINE, Viber, KakaoTalk, and the whole Facebook, Instagram and Messenger
apps), always or outside its allowed hours, while the rest of its internet
keeps working. Blocking messaging also cuts every push notification
on the device, including iMessage and incoming FaceTime calls, for as long as
it lasts.

A device can likewise be denied live online games, always or outside its
allowed hours. The block goes by the kind of traffic games send: UDP to the
internet is cut except on the few ports calls, Wi-Fi Calling, the web and the
clock need, games' own TCP ports are cut, and the games on a list the parent
edits, which starts with the games the plugin ships, are blocked by their
servers' addresses. Calls, Wi-Fi Calling, the web, video and app downloads keep
working.

WWW: https://github.com/nycoagung/opnsense-plugin-parentalcontrol

Plugin Changelog
----------------

0.4.0

* A Games tab holds the games whose servers are blocked for every device denied
  gaming. It starts with the games the plugin ships, all switched on; the parent
  adds, edits, deletes and switches games, each with its host names and address
  ranges, and a change takes effect at the next sync
* A release never changes or removes a game the list holds: it only adds the
  games it newly ships, and a shipped game the parent deleted does not come back.
  A new game whose name or host the list already has is left out and reported
  once in the system log
* Each game shows what it is blocked by and what it blocked at the last refresh,
  or why nothing
* Learned game-server addresses are remembered per host, so a game switched off
  or deleted stops being blocked at the next sync, and a host whose lookup fails
  keeps its addresses for up to 7 days
* No game blocks an answer of the messaging block's protected names, an address
  in a shared front end (the shared CDNs, Google's own front ends, Firebase
  Hosting, Vercel, Netlify, GitHub Pages) or a destination never treated as
  gaming, and a game with a host on a shared hosting service or a range that
  overlaps a guarded one cannot be saved
* Upgrading drops 0.3.0's learned game-server addresses, which no host was known
  to have given; the first sync learns them again
* tools/check-gaming-hosts.py counts a game publisher's own domains as the
  game's, and can resolve through a chosen DNS server (--resolver)
* tools/build-front-end-ranges.py generates Google's front ends

0.3.0

* A Gaming setting on every device: Not blocked, Blocked, or Blocked outside
  allowed hours, decided exactly as Messaging is
* A device denied gaming cannot send UDP to the internet except on a short
  safe-list (name lookups, time, the web over QUIC, Wi-Fi Calling, call relays,
  FaceTime, Zoom, Meet, Signal), cannot use games' TCP ports, and cannot reach
  the servers of Pokemon GO, Monster Hunter Now, Honkai Impact 3rd, Infinity
  Nikki, Love and Deepspace, Last War: Survival, MONOPOLY GO!, Gossip Harbor and
  the Heart of Vegas, Lightning Link and Cashman casino games, Niantic's sign-in,
  which every Niantic game uses, or Vivox's in-game voice chat. Whiteout
  Survival and Kingshot are blocked by their TCP ports
* UDP to the local network, the firewall itself, Discord's own network and the
  messaging apps' own networks is never treated as gaming, so calls, a VPN back
  home and Discord voice keep working
* A game host's address is learned only when it is the name's own and public; an
  answer through a CDN is left alone, except Last War's Cloudflare game gateway,
  which has an address of its own
* No device is blocked from gaming until the never-blocked table holds the whole
  local network, nor while a gaming alias is renamed by hand or missing
* The Status tab and the status JSON show gaming per device and in a summary line
* Table loads check every entry first and report a failed pfctl call, and only a
  small change falls back to configd address by address
* Every range is checked before it reaches a servers table, the shipped ones and
  a download kept from an earlier version included
* The messaging data build stops behind a filtering or failing resolver and on a
  per-site list that shrank
* tools/check-gaming-hosts.py checks the game hosts before a release

0.2.0

* Messaging blocking offers Viber, KakaoTalk, and Facebook & Instagram, which
  blocks the whole Facebook, Instagram and Messenger apps. KakaoTalk also
  blocks other Kakao services such as Daum and Kakao Map
* All eight apps are selected by default. Upgrading adds the three new ones to
  a saved selection, so a device denied messaging loses them too; a selection
  saved empty stays empty
* A protected name can belong to an app: Facebook's and Instagram's main names,
  and three of WhatsApp's, are protected only while their app is not selected
* The apps' names are looked up in turns, so a long list cannot starve a short
  one
* Viber's and KakaoTalk's networks ship with the plugin, built from ARIN's
  registry and from the prefixes Kakao announces
* The Settings tab has no Save button any more: Apply saves the settings when
  they changed, then applies them, and an invalid setting stops it
* A downloaded range that reaches into private, local, carrier-grade NAT or
  multicast address space is ignored, and the data generator refuses an app
  range that reaches into any special-purpose block
* WhatsApp's fallback chat name is blocked too: once its main chat server is
  blocked, WhatsApp moves to it within a minute or two
* Signal's current apps send messages through grpc.chat.signal.org, which is
  now on its list; Snapchat's chat gateway, duplex connection and API variants
  and LINE's backup, regional and media gateways are added too
* Telegram's apps have an address built in from a block Telegram leaves out of
  its published list: that block, 95.161.64.0/20, ships with the plugin and is
  blocked with the downloaded list
* LINE is blocked by its own network (AS38631), like KakaoTalk, which also
  blocks LINE's other services on blocked devices
* Facebook & Instagram lists every gateway, live-message and encrypted-chat
  server family Meta's apps use, with their fallbacks, and Facebook Lite;
  WhatsApp's media fallback is listed too
* WhatsApp and Facebook & Instagram are blocked at every Meta site: the
  addresses of their servers at all of Meta's sites ship with the plugin, so an
  app that Meta's DNS sends to a far-away site is still blocked
* Changes to the servers table are loaded with one pfctl call each, so
  thousands of entries apply at once

0.1.0

* Messaging blocking: each device has a Messaging setting (not blocked,
  blocked, or blocked outside its allowed hours) and Settings has a list of
  messaging apps to block. While a device is denied messaging all its
  notifications are cut too, including iMessage and incoming FaceTime calls
* Messaging adds two aliases (NoMessaging and Messaging_Servers) and two
  floating block rules, made the first time a device uses it. The internet
  alias can no longer be named NoMessaging or Messaging_Servers
* The Status tab shows messaging per device, with a summary line, and the
  status output gains the messaging fields
* A device address with host bits set (192.0.2.10/24) is used in its network
  form and one entered as /32 as the bare address, which is how pf keeps them
* Uninstall also removes the messaging aliases, rules and pf tables and the
  plugin's data directory, waits for a running sync, and refuses, changing
  nothing, while anything else in the configuration refers to one of the
  plugin's aliases
* An installed installer now runs the downloaded release's own installer, so a
  release that adds files installs completely. Upgrading from 0.0.2 needs the
  install run twice (see the README)

0.0.2

* Model fields are read through their typed accessors, and the scripts and
  API actions declare their types. Nothing visible changes
* The status output gives each device's enabled flag as true or false
  rather than "1" or "0"
* Hardening from static analysis: a DHCP lease line that cannot be split is
  skipped, only text addresses are taken from the pf table listing, and a
  status answer that is not a JSON object is reported as a failure

0.0.1

* Initial release
